- Order!

00:08:52.755 --> 00:08:55.255
(indistinct)

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:06.090
- Chaplain Sharon Kugler,
Yale University Chaplain

00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:08.103
will now give the invocation.

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:12.573
- Good morning.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:17.220
Please join me in the spirit of prayer.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:20.643
Almighty God, source of all truth.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:25.320
Here we stand humbled by
the breadth of service

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.610
both past and present in our midst.

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.280
We stand proud of what
has been made possible

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:35.910
because we have committed
ourselves to making your world

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:39.210
a better place through
our willing spirits,

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:42.840
able bodies and courageous hearts.

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.530
Although today is a
celebration and rightly so,

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:49.410
we remember those who have
given their lives in service

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:52.260
to their country and we hold close those

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:56.130
who are currently deployed
and ask that your gentle hand

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:59.793
protect them and guide them safely home.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:04.560
Now, may our hearts move
from gratitude to inspiration

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.050
as your divine spirit empowers us

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:10.440
with a renewed vision and purpose.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:14.550
Let us begin this review
lifted by splendor,

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.310
ignited by camaraderie.

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:21.390
Together in our many
voices, strengths and faiths

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:24.753
as your loving people, we say, amen.

00:10:27.559 --> 00:10:28.890
- Please be seated.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.920
The President's Review
has several purposes.

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:34.830
First, it serves to honor
and thank the leadership

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:38.790
of the universities that
host and support our units.

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:41.310
This event also serves as an opportunity

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:44.430
to publicly recognize the
achievements of the midshipmen

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:47.730
and cadets you see here
today who have distinguished

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:50.250
themselves during the academic year.

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:54.600
Ladies and gentlemen, Commanding
Officer Yale University

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.863
Naval Reserve Officer Training
Corps, Captain Ron Withrow.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:01.440
- Good morning everyone.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:06.870
President Salovey, General
Raymond, fellow ROTC commanders,

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.060
ROTC representatives
from our partner colleges

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.910
and universities, Provost
Strobel, Dean Lewis,

00:11:11.910 --> 00:11:13.950
Yale administrators, faculty and staff,

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:17.520
alumni and supporters who I
will collectively refer to

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.720
as friends of ROTC, award
presenters representing a host

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.790
of organizations, friends and families,

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:26.853
welcome all to the Yale
ROTC President's Review.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:31.655
This is a great opportunity
to showcase our Yale ROTC

00:11:31.655 --> 00:11:34.260
midshipmen and cadets,
recognize our top performers

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:37.800
and also recognize our
graduating class of 2023

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:41.040
who'll be commissioning
in just a few short weeks.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:42.810
This event wouldn't be
possible without the hard work

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.670
from several outstanding individuals.

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:46.590
I want to especially thank
Marine Option Midshipman

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:49.320
Third Class Evan Quang, the
Midshipman Officer in Charge

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:51.600
responsible for planning this grand event,

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:53.820
US Marine Corps Gunnery Sergeant Chaz King

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.060
for providing drill
instructor grade instruction.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:59.250
Our senior administrative
coordinator, Liz Vildaki

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:02.310
for coordinating with numerous
supporting organizations,

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:05.460
and our MC, US Marine
Corps Captain Ratzy May,

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:06.873
and many, many others.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:10.500
Before I introduce our reviewing
officer President Salovey

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:13.260
for his remarks, I want to talk
to you a bit about history,

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:14.613
duty and leadership.

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.580
For those of you seeing this
for the first time today

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:20.250
the pass in review consists
of an award ceremony

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:22.650
and a military pass in review.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:25.860
So first, a bit of history
regarding the passenger review

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:29.043
to expound on what the MC
Captain May has already stated.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:31.770
The pass in review is
a formal time-honored

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:34.470
ceremony dates back to
before the Civil War.

00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:36.660
The purpose of the ceremony
was to formally present

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:39.240
the military command to a new commander

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:40.560
or commanding officer.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.980
Probably visible to most of you would be

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:45.810
the pass in review
before the newly sworn in

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.870
president of the United
States, our Commander in Chief,

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.360
which follows the inaugural ceremony.

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.280
Another example would be
the pass in review conducted

00:12:53.280 --> 00:12:56.010
at the recruit graduation
ceremonies marking the completion

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:58.920
of bootcamp and enlistment
into active duty.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:02.130
One in which this guy
participated in 1985 by the way,

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:04.260
and yet I retain my youthful appearance

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:05.910
or at least I keep telling myself that

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:08.370
when I look in the mirror every morning.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:10.830
The military pass in review
has transformed over time

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:12.690
to also include honoring
the hard work and dedication

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:14.820
of military members in the
performance of their duties

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:16.830
as we will do today.

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:19.830
So what are the duties
of cadets and midshipmen?

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:22.140
They're not only college students
but distinguish themselves

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.420
by committing to serve their
country as future soldiers,

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.063
sailors, marines, airmen and guardians.

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:29.880
They carry the same course
load that other students

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.610
on campus carry, participating
in and leading the same

00:13:32.610 --> 00:13:35.490
diverse palette of extracurricular
activities, sports,

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.700
student, government
affinity groups, etc., etc.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:40.770
But they're different in
that they have taken an oath

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.530
to serve their country in
preparation to defend the freedoms

00:13:43.530 --> 00:13:47.010
and the liberties that
all US citizens enjoy.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:48.720
Committing to undergoing
intensive instruction

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:53.220
to prepare them and in fact
transform them mentally, morally

00:13:53.220 --> 00:13:56.940
and physically into warriors
in the profession of arms.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.430
And now looking
specifically at our seniors

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:01.350
who will be commissioning
in just a few short weeks.

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:04.200
I know each of you have been
giving considerable thought

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.080
to the oath of office that you will take.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:09.960
As current Navy Service Chief
Admiral Mike Gilday stated,

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:11.850
the oath is what binds us together.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.340
It is the foundation of our profession.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:17.220
It is our north star, it defines us.

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:20.130
Wise words that apply
equally to all because cadets

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:22.800
and midshipmen, no matter
the color of the uniform,

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.800
the oath is the same
and it will define you.

00:14:25.800 --> 00:14:27.930
And ladies and gentlemen,
looking at the world we live in

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.390
today, we need these creative
and energetic young men

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:33.480
and women more than ever
to lead our armed forces

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.940
in the coming critical years.

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:37.680
Last year, Vice Admiral Roy Kitchener,

00:14:37.680 --> 00:14:39.570
he's the head of our surface Navy,

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.360
hosted a junior officer training
symposium that was titled

00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:45.840
Embracing Ingenuity to
Sharpen Our Competitive Edge.

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.180
But this wasn't simply training
for these young leaders.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.130
They weren't in receive only mode.

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.860
Admiral Kitchener used the
symposium to foster wide ranging

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:54.960
discussion centered on
building efficiencies

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.840
within the force, improving
business practices,

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.700
and as a venue for junior
officers to present

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:02.760
current challenges in the
surface force that they saw

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:05.160
along with proposed solutions.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:06.000
Why?

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:08.310
Because as the Admiral
stated, junior officers play

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:11.313
an integral role in solving
problems on the waterfront.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.580
Commandant of the US Marine
Corps General David Berger

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:17.310
shared similar sentiments when
he stated small unit leaders

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:20.220
who are well trained, who
have experience and maturity

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:22.470
to make decisions and are
empowered to make decisions

00:15:22.470 --> 00:15:24.810
in lieu of detailed guidance are powerful

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:27.330
even when outnumbered and even
when up against formations

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:29.700
that are two, three, four times as big.

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:32.460
This is the bread and
butter of the Marine Corps.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:34.470
I acknowledge these are
naval centric examples

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.450
that I've given, but the
Navy and Marine Corps

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.480
certainly doesn't hold a
monopoly on challenging problems

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:40.980
and the need to solve them.

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.990
I'm sure all service branches
agree that we must look

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.360
to these enterprising,
energetic and creative young men

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:46.950
and women for solutions.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.140
Whether we are talking
about the Navy's get real,

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:51.900
get better mindset, the
Army's people first,

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.510
winning matters
transformational philosophy

00:15:54.510 --> 00:15:56.730
or the Air Force's
accelerate, change or lose

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:59.220
strategic approach, at the end of the day,

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.500
without intelligent, creative
committed and empowered

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:04.110
leaders manning our
enlisted and officer ranks,

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:07.290
they're just cliched empty catchphrases.

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:10.200
It takes real concern, real
leadership and a willingness

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:14.220
to get into the fight, make
mistakes and try, try again.

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:16.230
So to the cadets and
midshipmen standing here

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.060
on behalf of all the commanders present,

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:22.560
I charge each of you to
be the transformational

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:27.150
junior leaders and decision
makers that our nation needs.

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:29.430
Because good leadership does
not just propel the military

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:33.240
forward, it also propels our
nation and our world forward

00:16:33.240 --> 00:16:35.760
and we at ROTC enjoy a
great partnership with Yale

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:36.750
in this regard.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.450
Working together to develop intelligent,

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:41.910
caring and collaborative
leaders to transform

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:43.440
a world for the better.

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.300
Critical to this partnership
has been the leadership

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:48.630
and the guiding vision of
President Peter Salovey.

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:51.450
Yale's mission statement reads
in part that Yale educates

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:55.950
aspiring leaders worldwide who
serve all sectors of society.

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:58.320
One can easily see the
similarity in missions

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.090
and how this contributes to a supportive,

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:01.890
effective partnership.

00:17:01.890 --> 00:17:04.560
This is no doubt a direct
reflection of President Salovey's

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.540
inspirational leadership and vision.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.490
So at this time I would like to introduce

00:17:08.490 --> 00:17:10.440
our reviewing official and host,

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:13.170
the 23rd president of Yale
University and the Chris Argyris

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:16.993
Professor of Psychology,
Peter Salovey. (claps)

00:17:16.993 --> 00:17:19.223
(audience claps)

00:17:19.223 --> 00:17:20.276
- Thank you so much.

00:17:20.276 --> 00:17:21.609
Appreciate that.

00:17:30.203 --> 00:17:31.350
Thank you Captain Withrow.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:34.890
I appreciate the kind
introduction and I'd also like

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:38.910
to thank Colonel Granholm
for his inspired leadership

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:43.383
of Yale's Air Force Reserve
Officers Training Corps unit.

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:47.340
Both Captain Withrow and Colonel Granholm

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:49.893
will be retiring after this semester.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:55.080
And so before I proceed any
further, I want to offer

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:58.410
my heartfelt appreciation
and that of a grateful

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:03.090
Yale community for both
of your commitments

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:06.330
to advancing stability in the world.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:09.450
As we wish you both the
very best for the wonderful

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:12.360
new chapter on which
you are about to embark,

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:16.380
we know that you will
remain very much a part

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:17.673
of the Yale family.

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:20.160
With the approach of their retirements,

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:25.050
please join me in recognizing
Captain Ron Withrow

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:28.181
and Colonel George Granholm's
exceptional service

00:18:28.181 --> 00:18:30.820
to this country. (claps)

00:18:30.820 --> 00:18:33.570
(audience claps)

00:18:42.971 --> 00:18:43.804
Thank you.

00:18:43.804 --> 00:18:46.473
They have been wonderful
leaders here on our campus.

00:18:47.310 --> 00:18:50.670
To all the parents,
loved ones, active duty

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:55.670
military members, veterans
here, I extend a warm greeting

00:18:56.310 --> 00:18:59.253
on behalf of my colleagues
across the university.

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:04.410
And I wish to welcome cadets,
liaisons and leaders here

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:08.460
today from our Air Force
affiliated universities,

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:11.010
Fairfield University,
Quinnipiac University,

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:12.840
Sacred Heart University,

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.570
Southern Connecticut State University,

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:18.630
University of New Haven,
Wesleyan University

00:19:18.630 --> 00:19:21.573
and Western Connecticut State University.

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:26.310
The sense of service we mark
on this historic occasion

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:30.180
dates to the founding of our institution

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:31.443
and of the republic.

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:34.260
Here are just a few examples.

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:39.180
In 1701, Yale became the
first American University

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:44.180
to inscribe service to society explicitly

00:19:44.280 --> 00:19:46.053
in its founding legislation.

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:50.190
Scores of Yaleys have
served with distinction

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:54.333
in every major conflict
since the Revolutionary War.

00:19:55.950 --> 00:19:59.040
Yale Air Force ROTC
was one of the original

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:04.040
77 senior air ROTC units
established by an order

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:06.513
General Eisenhower signed.

00:20:08.591 --> 00:20:11.220
And Yale was also among the
initial group of universities

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:15.600
to establish an ROTC program
more than a century ago.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:20.600
Army ROTC in 1917, followed
by Naval ROTC in 1926

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:26.463
and the Air Force ROTC in 1946.

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:33.750
At the time, Major General
Robert M. Danford predicted

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:38.460
the program would become one
of the many priceless heritages

00:20:38.460 --> 00:20:42.120
of future generations at Yale.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:47.120
When I look out at all of you,
I can see that he was right.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:52.710
The 55 Air Force cadets
and the 31 midshipmen

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:56.490
currently participating
in Yale's ROTC program

00:20:56.490 --> 00:21:01.473
are carrying forward the proud
legacy of those before them.

00:21:02.490 --> 00:21:06.240
This includes the valor of
nearly 30,000 Yaleys who served

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:10.593
in the First and Second world
wars, Korea and Vietnam.

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:16.080
And the spirit of sacrifice
that links these military men

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:21.063
and women is a hallmark of
the spirit of Yale itself.

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:26.943
It is a desire to serve, to
look beyond self-interest.

00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:31.660
It is the desire to
contribute to the common good

00:21:32.670 --> 00:21:36.640
and it is in short the desire
to improve the world today

00:21:37.740 --> 00:21:40.530
and for future generations.

00:21:40.530 --> 00:21:44.770
Indeed, that is how our
mission statement begins

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:50.810
and this is the call the
class of 2023 has answered.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:53.997
Your decision to serve our country

00:21:53.997 --> 00:21:57.900
and the armed forces is
vital at this moment.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:22:00.300
We can see the increasing pressure

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:03.663
of the spirit of division
around the globe.

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:08.080
Yet today we salute your accomplishments

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:14.283
and we can also see a
thriving spirit of service.

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:17.880
It's a spirit forged over centuries.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:22.380
Indeed, the fabric of our
nation is woven by individuals

00:22:22.380 --> 00:22:26.583
who have defended it for nearly 250 years.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.540
Their resilience and wisdom
helped build a nation

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:34.980
and their strength helped bind it together

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:36.633
during difficult times.

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:43.220
Their courage helped save a
world torn apart by war twice.

00:22:44.790 --> 00:22:47.190
Their determination has protected millions

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:50.463
from a life of oppression and hardship.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:54.600
Their compassion has brought
food, clothing, shelter

00:22:54.600 --> 00:22:58.563
and medicine to those who needed it most.

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:03.120
And their unselfish motives
in the face of adversity

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:06.333
brought hope to those who are suffering.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:12.840
Throughout your time, I've
observed the same sense

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:17.840
of optimism, courage,
and selflessness in you.

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:22.680
And I know the class of 2023 will manage

00:23:22.680 --> 00:23:27.213
to make us even more
proud in the years ahead.

00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:34.050
Now I'd like to congratulate
all our soon-to-be graduates

00:23:34.050 --> 00:23:37.860
and express my appreciation
to all our midshipmen

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:41.160
and cadets for their dedication to service

00:23:41.160 --> 00:23:44.730
and their many contributions to Yale

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:46.863
and their universities and colleges.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:51.990
You'll soon be second
lieutenants and ensigns

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:56.823
serving as America's
ambassadors around the world.

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:01.170
You'll face challenges and opportunities

00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:04.200
in a rapidly changing global climate

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:07.890
with incredible technological
advances that both connect

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:11.970
people across nations and cultural borders

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:16.353
but also pose unpredictable
security risks.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:19.980
You'll also experience shifts in political

00:24:19.980 --> 00:24:22.830
and environmental landscapes.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:26.550
Yet regardless of what
trials you may face,

00:24:26.550 --> 00:24:31.290
I am confident that you, like
the generations of Yaleys

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:34.740
who contributed to this
country before you,

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:37.863
will lead and serve the greater good.

00:24:39.030 --> 00:24:41.937
We're so proud of what
you have accomplished here

00:24:41.937 --> 00:24:46.020
and we eagerly look forward
to all you will achieve

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:49.530
in the future as you
build on Yale's tradition

00:24:49.530 --> 00:24:54.530
of loyal service to
community and to country.

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:59.110
Thank you very much for joining us today

00:25:01.093 --> 00:25:02.583
and to all of you thank you,

00:25:03.785 --> 00:25:08.160
and please accept my most
heartfelt congratulations.

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:09.300
Thank you very much.

