00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:06.450
United States Marine
Corps and Marine Fellow

00:33:06.450 --> 00:33:09.210
at the Jackson School of Global Affairs.

00:33:09.210 --> 00:33:12.870
Corporal Ricketts graduated
from Yale College in 1916

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:15.300
and was killed in action
during World War I

00:33:15.300 --> 00:33:17.850
at Mont Blanc in 1918.

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:20.130
A decorated Marine who was the recipient

00:33:20.130 --> 00:33:23.010
of the Croix de Guerre, all
accounts maintained that

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:25.800
Corporal Ricketts had a knack
for maintaining his wits

00:33:25.800 --> 00:33:27.600
in the face of chaos.

00:33:27.600 --> 00:33:30.210
Importantly, he was an enlisted man,

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:32.520
a non-commissioned officer.

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:35.400
NCOs are the backbone of the Marine Corps.

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:39.090
More than any other service
the Marines place special trust

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:41.550
and confidence in the
abilities of their junior

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:43.710
most enlisted leadership.

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.140
It is this sort of
Marine that the recipient

00:33:46.140 --> 00:33:48.510
of the Corporal Langdon
Laws Ricketts Award

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:51.630
will be called upon to lead
shortly after their graduation

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:53.100
from Yale College.

00:33:53.100 --> 00:33:56.223
